-- title:
--   Injectivity of the q-expansion of differentials on K(j,j_N)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $N$ be a prime, let $L$ be a field with a $K$-algebra structure, and write $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldC K N` for the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $K((q))$ obtained by adjoining to $K$ the two elements `jqModC K` and `jqNModC K N`; here `jqModC K` is the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot(E_4^3\eta^{-24})$, the image over $K$ of the integral power series `jNum`$=$ `eisenstein4`$^3\cdot$`dedekindEtaUnitInv` multiplied by the monomial $q^{-1}$, and `jqNModC K N` is its image under the substitution $q \mapsto q^N$ (`qExpand K N`). Let $\sigma : F \to L((q))$ be a homomorphism of $K$-algebras, and let `thetaL L` denote the $L$-linear operator $q\,d/dq$ on $L((q))$, that is $f \mapsto q \cdot f'$. Assume that `thetaL L` applied to $\sigma$ of the generator `jqModC K`, viewed as an element of $F$, is nonzero. Then the $K$-linear map `qExpansionDiffAlong σ` from $\Omega_{F/K}$ to $L((q))$ — the chosen linear map $\varphi$ with $\varphi(d x) =$ `thetaL L`$(\sigma x)$ for all $x \in F$ and $\varphi(f\cdot\omega) = \sigma(f)\,\varphi(\omega)$, if such a map exists, and $0$ otherwise — is injective.
--
--   This is the function-field form of the $q$-expansion principle for differentials on the modular curve of level $N$: the module of Kähler differentials of the field generated by the $q$-expansions of $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$ is detected by $q$-expansions along any embedding at which $q\,dj/dq$ survives. It is used in the analysis of the Hecke action on differentials and on the Picard group, via [`ModularCurve.eq_zero_or_exists_eq_nsmul_of_heckePic0Fibre_eq_eisenstein_of_heckeOperatorModL_eq_of_smul_eq_neg`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eq_zero_or_exists_eq_nsmul_of_heckePic0Fibre_eq_eisenstein_of_heckeOperatorModL_eq_of_smul_eq_neg).
